      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the safety stock calculation using standard deviation, which is a more precise way to account for variability in demand and lead time.\n\nSafety Stock = Z-Score x Standard Deviation of Lead Time Demand\n\nThe Z-Score represents the number of standard deviations from the mean you want to cover. A common Z-Score to use is 1.65, which covers approximately 95% of the demand variability, giving you a good balance between having enough stock and not overstocking.\n\nTo calculate the Standard Deviation of Lead Time Demand, you'll need historical data on your daily demand and lead time variability. Once you have that, you can calculate the standard deviation for the lead time demand.\n\nLet's say your average daily demand is 100 battery cells, and the standard deviation of your daily demand is 20 cells. If your lead time is 10 days, the standard deviation of lead time demand would be:\n\nStandard Deviation of Lead Time Demand = Standard Deviation of Daily Demand x √Lead Time\n= 20 x √10\n= 20 x 3.16\n= 63.2 cells\n\nNow, using a Z-Score of 1.65, your safety stock would be",
